Date: Fri, 16 Apr 93 13:57:57 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Dorothy Denning's friends strike

As I read and re-read the document, it occurs to me that it may not be
necessary (for their purposes) to ban strong encryption immediately.

Their emphasis is on voice communication, and AT&T has already
announced (according to an AP story) that they are looking forward to
designing in the Clipper so that they will have a standard encryption
method across their whole range of products.

The government's aims could perhaps be met by having Clipper encryption
become the standard for voice communication.  One fact about any form of
encrypted communication is that the parties at each end need compatible
encryption systems.  If you want routine voice communication then you
must be sure that the people you call have encrypting telephones which
will work with yours.  This points to the need for a standard; otherwise
users of AT&T phones won't be able to talk to users of Motorola phones,
etc.

The government may want to get Clipper into place as a de facto standard
(or possibly even an official one).  Once this happens, anyone wanting
to sell a non-Clipper phone would have a very difficult time because so
few other phones would be usable with it.  This might explain, too, why
they are keeping the encryption standard secret.  Otherwise it would be
easy to make a Clipper-compatible phone which did not have the back
door keys.

If this is in fact their approach, it gives us more time to think about
getting alternative systems into place.  For years, it seems, I've been
reading proposals for systems which would digitize voice, compress it to
a small bit rate like 9600 bps, encrypt it, and transmit via modem.  If
someone already had a PC with a modem and a sound card they'd have pretty
much all the hardware needed.  The problem has always been getting enough
computing power to do the compression in real time.  Perhaps in the next
couple of years as 486 and Pentium PC's become popular we will finally
have enough power that freeware secure voice communication software will
be possible.

Hal

