Date: Fri, 16 Apr 93 14:19:32 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: CRYPT/LAW: Can We Estimate Wiretap Value?

Perry sounds the alarm:
>Seriously folks, quit what you are doing and start researching this ..

OK, I'll bite.  

So the US Government is serious about ensuring it can "wiretap"
private communication, eh?  I don't know if anyone else has done this,
but I think a great piece of rhetorical ammunition would be to create
a quotable study estimating the monetary benefit of having state
wiretap abilities.  Imagine the headline: "CATO study says wiretaps
worth only $10M/yr to police.  Will we sell our privacy for trinkets?"

Here's the idea: A wiretap law is handy in that it reduces the
resources that must be spend by police to raise the probability of
catching any given criminal to any given level.  Wiretaps make law
enforcement cheaper.  But the question is: how much cheaper?

Higher probabilities of catching criminals can also come by having
more detectives spend longer investigating cases.  And even when
probabilities can't be raised, equivalent deterrence can come by
raising the fine/punishment required of criminals who are caught.
(This raises social costs because of the risk aversion of criminals,
and of innocents who are falsely convicted.)

Even if a lack of wiretaps doubled the cost to reach a given
probability of conviction level, the value of wiretaps would seem to
be bounded by about total amount spent investigating crimes where
wiretaps were used.  I'd be surprised if this were > $100M/yr.  

This benefit could be compared to estimates of the cost of everyone
using government standard encryption chips, etc., vs. the no doubt
more efficient and cheaper chips they would have otherwise used.  Any
remaining benefit would then be what we should ask citizens to compare
to their personal value of being secure against the risks of illegal
government spying against them.  (Headline: "Is freedom from state
snooping worth more than five cents a year to you?")

So what we want is to ASAP get someone who knows the literature on the
economics of law enforcement to do a study estimating the value of
wiretaps.  Maybe Tom Bell or David Friedman would know who to suggest,
or perhaps be interested themselves.  I'm just an amateur in the econ
of law enforcement, but might be talked into giving it a try (and
remember: money talks :-).  Of course to be credible, I'd have to
commit to publicizing my results regardless of the number they
estimate.  If the value turns out to be $10B/yr., I'd have to say so.

On a more idealistic level, I'll note that with the private law
enforcement scheme I've proposed (paper available), where criminals
are indirectly placed in charge of hiring enforcers, people would
internalize these tradeoffs.  Each person would choose whether or not
to use communication media with trapdoors to allow wiretaps, and
enforcers would set fines contingent on whether the convicted criminal
allowed wiretaps.  By choosing no wiretaps, you would know that you
would be fined more, should you ever be convicted of a crime where
wiretaps might have been useful in finding you.

