that I know of in which the non-contradiction principle doesn't hold is
the world of politics. ;-)

   In every day language, terms are not specific enough to apply
rigorous logic to, so it's kind of meaningless to evaluate them in
the context of Aristotelian logic. The world of mathematics is a completely
different picture. There is only a very small group of mathematicians 
in academia who believe in what you have stated (the intuitionists, no
law of excluded middle).  In particular, they don't believe in proof
by contradiction. However, they have never demonstrated a single
indirect proof to be false (by providing a counter example). I am
suspicous of anyone who claims that mathematics/logic has invisible
flaws since it has withstood the test of time against much greater geniuses
than those making the claims. I'd much rather see evidence.

