From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: LP poll results from Georgia

[From _CLM News_, April 1993]

POLLING IN GEORGIA: WHAT THE PUBLIC THINKS ABOUT THE LP

	Tired of guessing about what the public thinks about the
Libertarian Party, the Georgia LP contracted with a professional
polling firm to sample public opinion.  The poll was conducted
in December by the highly respected Washington, DC polling firm
of Fabrizio, McLaughlin & Associates as part of a joint project
with the Georgia Public Policy Foundation, and had a margin of
error of plus or minus 4.9% with a 95% confidence level.

	The Georgia LP was startled to find an extraordinarily
high level of recognition of the Libertarian Party.  Of course
the Georgia LP had a very high profile during the Coverdell-
Fowler Senate run-off election.  But, the traditional Libertarian
attitude was that our lack of success was because the public had
never heard about us.  Yet, the poll showed that a phenomenal 77%
of Georgians knew enough about the LP to form an opinion about us.
This was a higher recognition rate than for the current Lt.
Governor or Attorney General.


HIGH UNFAVORABLES.

	Unfortunately, the opinion that they had formed about the
LP is a negative one.  According to the poll results, only 24.3%
of Georgians had a favorable opinion of Libertarians, while 53%
had an unfavorable opinion.  Interestingly, the LP was more
popular among self-described liberals (36.8%F, 45.6%U) than among
conservatives (19.4%F, 63.6%U).  In general, the LP did better
among younger voters and lower-income voters.  Perot voters were
also a relatively strong group (33.3%F, 39.4%U).  Very weak groups
included: regular churchgoers (27.3%F, 63.6%U); Bush voters
(18.9%F, 62.3%U); the elderly (19.6%F, 51%U); "Reagan Democrats"
(28%F, 68%U); and middle-income earners (13.8%F, 67.7%U).  In
general, the LP seems to be missing the middle class. [...in Georgia.]


NO ISSUE IDENTIFICATION.

	One particularly disappointing finding of the poll was that
the LP was not strongly identified with any issue except drugs/crime.
For example, while we think of ourselves as the anti-tax party, those
Georgians who thought cutting taxes was the most important issue to
them were no more likely to have a favorable opinion of the LP.  The
same was true of other issues such as school choice, opposition to
socialized medicine, privatization, etc.  Apparently the LP has not
been able to focus its message sufficiently to become identified
with these issues in the minds of the voters.


A LIBERTARIAN CONSTITUENCY.

	The poll did contain evidence of hope for the LP's future.
There was strong public support for the LP's position on most basic
issues.  For example, 56% of Georgians supported greater privatization
of state services; 88.5% of Georgians favored a requirement of a 2/3
super-majority before taxes could be raised; 54% opposed a government-
run national health care system; and 57% supported school choice.
These are issues that should be natural for the LP.  At the same time,
most voters pro-choice and rejected "conservative" positions on social
issues.  By building on public support for the issues where the public
agrees with us, the Libertarian Party should be able to change public
opinion and become a major player in Georgian (and American) politics.


_Complete poll results are available upon request._ [from CLM]


