   On a side note, a friend of mine and I have been designing
v32bis voice encryption software. It's crude, but it should work.
I'm designing my end for an Amiga + cheap sampler, he is designing his
end on a 486 + Sound Blaster pro.

  Here's how we kludged it.
 
  Take 8khz 8bit samples and decimate them to 4khz 4/6-bit using
interpolation. This requires 2000/3000 cps. Using fibonacci delta compression,
another 50% can be compressed yielding a required 1000/1500 cps. The
resulting sound quality is very bad (hard to understand) but it still
works (a typical conversation would probably have lots of
"hey, could you repeat that?")  
  With ADCMP + v.terbo/codex, a lot more is possible.

  Right now, the design is half duplex (walkie-talkie like), and
only uses a multiple substitution cypher because most of the CPU
power is soaked up sampling/compressing/serial transfer. I don't plan
on finishing it till summer, however I have tested serial port/sampling
code and it works. I have also experimented with different 
decimation rates, 6bit @ 4khz seems to work ok, 4bit @ 4khz is a little
hard to understand.

  You can do some tests yourself even if you don't have a sampler
by downloading the publically available SOX (soundtools) software and
then applying it to internet talk radio samples.


  

